"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][3]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M3-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M3-L1 – Python for data analysis and numerical computing<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Programming and Data Processing<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M1; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>\nis helpful.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish Python data\nstructures and formulate a controlled numerical analysis as a\nfunction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data and types<\/h2>\n<p>A measurement station supplies the sequence 12, 18, missing, 24 (in\n°C). In Python, <code>None<\/code> stands for an absent value; it is not\na number. A list preserves order and may contain different types. A\ntuple has immutable entries, while a dictionary associates keys with\nvalues. A dictionary is suitable, for example, for a measurement record\nwith a station identifier and a temperature. A set contains unique\nelements without a guaranteed order relevant to the application. The\nchoice of structure depends on how it is accessed, not just on its\nmemory size.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Writing a function for analysis<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001671_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Four input values, one excluded missing value, a sum of 54 and a mean of 18.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four input values, one excluded missing\nvalue, a sum of 54 and a mean of 18.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: From measurement to indicator.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>We want to average only the measurements that are present. The\nfunction should not silently interpret an empty set of valid\nmeasurements as a temperature of zero.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> mittelwert(werte):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    gueltig <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [x <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> werte <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"va\">None<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> gueltig:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;Keine Messwerte&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(gueltig) <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(gueltig)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>werte <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">18<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">None<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">24<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(mittelwert(werte))  <span class=\"co\"># 18.0<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The list comprehension creates a new list. The condition is checked\nfor every entry. The local variable <code>gueltig<\/code> exists\nindependently of the original list; the function does not alter its\ncontents. The denominator is 3, not 4. The decision to exclude missing\nvalues must be documented: if the sensor fails precisely when\ntemperatures are high, the mean may be systematically too low.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Numerical computing and limits of error handling<\/h2>\n<p>NumPy is useful for homogeneous numerical arrays.\n<code>np.array([12, 18, 24], dtype=float)<\/code> creates an array of\nfloating-point numbers. <code>mean()<\/code> calculates its mean. A\nlibrary can execute the same calculation efficiently without changing\nits definition in the application domain. A Python integer can become\nmuch larger than a fixed-width integer in NumPy; the data type is\ntherefore part of the data contract. The Python code shown checks only\nfor <code>None<\/code>, not for strings, infinite values or\n<code>NaN<\/code>. Such cases must be checked explicitly in production\ndata. Floating-point rounding (<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L7<\/a>)\nremains relevant even when using libraries.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>A second station reports <code>[10, None, 20, 30]<\/code>. What does\nthe function return? What happens with <code>[None]<\/code>? How could\ncompleteness also be determined?<\/p>\n<p>Solution: 20.0; the second call raises a <code>ValueError<\/code>. For\nthe first list, completeness is 3/4 = 75%. This indicator should be\nstored independently of the mean. The completeness rate is also\nundefined for an empty input list.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.python.org/3.14/tutorial/datastructures.html\">Python\n3.14, Data Structures, Ch. 5<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending; access date recorded only in source register (2026-11-20);\nsubstantive check not evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://numpy.org/doc/2.3/user/basics.broadcasting.html\">NumPy\n2.3, Broadcasting<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending; access\ndate recorded only in source register (2026-11-20); substantive check\nnot evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M3-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M3-L2 – R for statistics and data analysis<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Programming and Data Processing<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M1; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L1<\/a>\nis helpful.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Use R vectors and data frames,\nand handle missing values and group analyses explicitly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>R as a statistical working environment<\/h2>\n<p>R is designed for analysis, modelling and visualisation. An atomic\nvector contains elements of a common type. <code>c(7, 11, NA, 15)<\/code>\ncreates a numerical vector with one missing entry. <code>NA<\/code> is\nnot the number zero; many calculations themselves return <code>NA<\/code>\nwhen a value is missing unless its treatment has been specified. Unlike\nPython, R indexes vectors from 1. <code>x[1]<\/code> is the first\nelement.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre class=\"sourceCode r\"><code class=\"sourceCode r\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x <span class=\"ot\">&lt;-<\/span> <span class=\"fu\">c<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">11<\/span>, <span class=\"cn\">NA<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">15<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"fu\">mean<\/span>(x, <span class=\"at\">na.rm =<\/span> <span class=\"cn\">TRUE<\/span>)  <span class=\"co\"># 11<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"fu\">sum<\/span>(<span class=\"sc\">!<\/span><span class=\"fu\">is.na<\/span>(x))         <span class=\"co\"># 3<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Here the parameter <code>na.rm<\/code> means that missing entries are\nexcluded before calculation. If all values are missing, the group should\nexplicitly be treated as unsuitable for analysis rather than assuming a\nmeaningful mean.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data frames and groups in the application domain<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001673_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Four measured times are split by workshop and averaged to 10 and 8 minutes respectively.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four measured times are split by workshop\nand averaged to 10 and 8 minutes respectively.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Grouping in R.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A data frame has columns of equal length, which may have different\ntypes. We record four processing times in minutes for two workshops.\nWith dplyr, the question can be expressed as a sequence of grouping and\nsummarising operations. The additional package must be installed.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb2\"><pre class=\"sourceCode r\"><code class=\"sourceCode r\"><span id=\"cb2-1\"><a href=\"#cb2-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"fu\">library<\/span>(dplyr)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-2\"><a href=\"#cb2-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>d <span class=\"ot\">&lt;-<\/span> <span class=\"fu\">data.frame<\/span>(<span class=\"at\">werk =<\/span> <span class=\"fu\">c<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;B&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;B&quot;<\/span>),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-3\"><a href=\"#cb2-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                <span class=\"at\">minuten =<\/span> <span class=\"fu\">c<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-4\"><a href=\"#cb2-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>d <span class=\"sc\">|&gt;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-5\"><a href=\"#cb2-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>  <span class=\"fu\">group_by<\/span>(werk) <span class=\"sc\">|&gt;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-6\"><a href=\"#cb2-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>  <span class=\"fu\">summarise<\/span>(<span class=\"at\">n =<\/span> <span class=\"fu\">n<\/span>(), <span class=\"at\">mittel =<\/span> <span class=\"fu\">mean<\/span>(minuten), <span class=\"at\">.groups =<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;drop&quot;<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The output contains A with two cases and 10 minutes, and B with two\ncases and 8 minutes. The pipe symbol <code>|&gt;<\/code> passes the\nresult of an expression as the first argument of the next function call.\nGrouping creates a new analysis here; it does not delete the original\nobservations. In a new analysis, the resulting table should report not\nonly a mean but also the corresponding count.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Statistical meaning before syntax<\/h2>\n<p>An overall mean is not automatically the mean of the group means. If\nA has two and B has four measurements, the four B values together carry\ntwice as much weight as the two A values together in an analysis per\nmeasurement. If each workshop is to have the same weight instead, an\nunweighted mean of the group means is the appropriate alternative\nindicator. The purpose of the analysis determines the choice.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Workshop A has times of 8 and 12 minutes; B has 6, 10, 10 and 14\nminutes. Calculate the overall mean and the equally weighted workshop\nmean. What distinguishes this example from a case with unequal workshop\nmeans?<\/p>\n<p>Solution: Both group means are 10, so both indicators are also 10. If\nthe last B value is changed from 14 to 20, the results are B = 11.5,\noverall mean = 11 and equally weighted workshop mean = 10.75. The equal\nresults in the first case were not a general arithmetic rule.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://r4ds.hadley.nz/workflow-basics.html\">R for Data\nScience (2e), Workflow: basics, Ch. 2<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending; access date recorded only in\nsource register (2026-11-20); substantive check not evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://r4ds.hadley.nz/data-transform.html\">R for Data\nScience (2e), Data transformation, Ch. 3<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending; access date recorded only in\nsource register (2026-11-20); substantive check not evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M3-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M3-L3 – Arrays, vectorisation and broadcasting<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Programming and Data Processing<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Check array shapes and\ndistinguish element-wise operations from matrix products.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Computing along axes<\/h2>\n<p>On a production line, two measured quantities are recorded for each\nof three components. A table with three rows and two columns can be\nrepresented as a NumPy array. An axis is a position within the array\nshape; in this application, axis 0 represents the components and axis 1\nthe measured quantities. <code>a.sum(axis=0)<\/code> aggregates the\ncomponents and returns two sums. <code>a.sum(axis=1)<\/code> aggregates\nthe measured quantities and returns three sums. The second result is\nmeaningful only if the quantities can be added.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Broadcasting as a shape rule<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001534_en.svg\"\nalt=\"A 3 × 2 matrix minus an offset vector of length 2 yields a 3 × 2 matrix.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A 3 × 2 matrix minus an offset vector of\nlength 2 yields a 3 × 2 matrix.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Broadcasting a column offset.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A known offset is to be subtracted for each measured quantity. A\nvector with two components acts on every row:<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>a <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([[<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>], [<span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">13<\/span>], [<span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">17<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>b <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>c <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> a <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> b<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(c)  <span class=\"co\"># [[3, 6], [5, 10], [7, 14]]<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>NumPy compares the dimensions from right to left. Two dimensions are\ncompatible if they are equal or one of them is 1; missing dimensions on\nthe left are treated as ones. Thus <code>(3, 2)<\/code> and\n<code>(2,)<\/code> are compatible. The expansion of the vector is\nconceptual; NumPy does not have to create three physical copies of it. A\nresulting array may nevertheless occupy a great deal of memory. A vector\nof shape <code>(3,)<\/code>, in contrast, is not compatible with\n<code>(3, 2)<\/code>. If one common offset is to apply to each row, shape\n<code>(3, 1)<\/code> is needed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Vectorisation, copies and products<\/h2>\n<p>For numerical arrays, vectorisation shifts many element-wise\noperations from Python loops into optimised library functions. It does\nnot promise constant runtime: the number of elements processed still\nmatters. Intermediate results can increase memory requirements. A slice\nsuch as <code>a[:, 0]<\/code> is a view of the original values in this\narray; changes may affect the original. If needed, <code>.copy()<\/code>\ncreates independent data. <code>a * b<\/code> performs element-wise\nmultiplication, while <code>a @ b<\/code> forms row-wise dot products\n(see <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Calculate <code>a - np.array([[1], [2], [3]])<\/code> and\n<code>a @ b<\/code>. What shapes do the results have?<\/p>\n<p>Solution: The first operation yields\n<code>[[4, 8], [5, 11], [6, 14]]<\/code> with shape <code>(3, 2)<\/code>.\nThe matrix-vector product yields <code>[37, 53, 69]<\/code> with shape\n<code>(3,)<\/code>. The multiplication sums weighted row entries, while\nbroadcasting subtracts one value in each row. For square arrays, check\nparticularly carefully whether a compatible vector actually corresponds\nto the intended axis.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://numpy.org/doc/2.3/user/basics.broadcasting.html\">NumPy\n2.3, Broadcasting<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending; access\ndate recorded only in source register (2026-11-20); substantive check\nnot evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://numpy.org/doc/2.3/user/basics.copies.html\">NumPy\n2.3, Copies and views<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending;\naccess date recorded only in source register (2026-11-20); substantive\ncheck not evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M3-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M3-L4 – Grouping, filtering and joining tabular data<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Programming and Data Processing<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L1<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L2<\/a>\nis helpful.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Apply table operations using\nkeys, groups and controlled cardinalities.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Tables as relationships<\/h2>\n<p>One table stores individual deliveries, while a second holds\nwarehouse reference data. A delivery ID identifies a delivery; the\nwarehouse ID may appear several times in the delivery table. In the\nreference table, however, it identifies exactly one warehouse. This\ncreates a many-to-one relationship. Row positions are not stable\nidentities because sorting and filtering can change them.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Joining and filtering<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001720_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Four deliveries are matched to two warehouse reference records; Z has no region.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four deliveries are matched to two\nwarehouse reference records; Z has no region.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Left join with an unmatched key.<\/em><\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> pandas <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> pd<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>l <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> pd.DataFrame({<span class=\"st\">&quot;lager&quot;<\/span>: [<span class=\"st\">&quot;X&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;X&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;Y&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;Z&quot;<\/span>],<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                  <span class=\"st\">&quot;kg&quot;<\/span>: [<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]})<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>s <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> pd.DataFrame({<span class=\"st\">&quot;lager&quot;<\/span>: [<span class=\"st\">&quot;X&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;Y&quot;<\/span>],<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                  <span class=\"st\">&quot;region&quot;<\/span>: [<span class=\"st\">&quot;Nord&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;Sued&quot;<\/span>]})<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>d <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> l.merge(s, on<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&quot;lager&quot;<\/span>, how<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&quot;left&quot;<\/span>,<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            validate<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&quot;many_to_one&quot;<\/span>, indicator<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(d.loc[d[<span class=\"st\">&quot;_merge&quot;<\/span>] <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;left_only&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;lager&quot;<\/span>].tolist())<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"co\"># [&#39;Z&#39;]<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>A left join preserves all deliveries. Warehouse Z has no matching\nreference record; its region field is missing. An inner join would omit\nZ. <code>validate<\/code> guards against unexpectedly repeated reference\nkeys. Care is needed with missing keys: pandas can join missing keys to\neach other, unlike typical SQL equality joins. A careful data contract\nprohibits missing or ambiguous reference keys or defines separate rules\nfor them.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Grouping, aggregating and checking<\/h2>\n<p>The following code block uses the <code>d<\/code> table created in the\npreceding example. Run the two examples in sequence.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb2\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb2-1\"><a href=\"#cb2-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>resultat <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (d.groupby(<span class=\"st\">&quot;region&quot;<\/span>, dropna<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">False<\/span>)[<span class=\"st\">&quot;kg&quot;<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-2\"><a href=\"#cb2-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>             .agg([<span class=\"st\">&quot;sum&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;size&quot;<\/span>]))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Nord (North) has a sum of 12 and a count of 2; Sued (South) has a sum\nof 4 and a count of 1; the unknown region has a sum of 2 and a count of\n1. The overall quantity remains 18 kg. A filter such as\n<code>d.loc[d[\"kg\"] &gt;= 5]<\/code> returns two rows; it changes the set\nunder consideration. A group summary, by contrast, changes the\ngranularity: deliveries become regions. With several grouping keys, one\nrow is formed for each combination. A pivot can arrange these results as\na cross-tabulation, but is not an additional observation. Key counts,\nrow counts and appropriate control totals should be compared before and\nafter a join.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose the reference data contain a second record for X. How many\nrows does the unchecked left join produce? What sum results? What\nhappens with validation?<\/p>\n<p>Solution: The two X deliveries each appear twice; together with Y and\nZ, this produces six rows. The sum becomes 30 kg instead of 18 kg.\n<code>validate=\"many_to_one\"<\/code> rejects the join. The correct remedy\nrequires clarification of the meaning of the key in the application\ndomain, not the indiscriminate deletion of arbitrary result rows.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/merging.html\">pandas,\nMerge, join, concatenate and compare<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending; access date recorded only in\nsource register (2026-11-20); substantive check not evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/groupby.html\">pandas,\nGroup by: split-apply-combine<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending; access date recorded only in source register (2026-11-20);\nsubstantive check not evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M3-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M3-L5 – Algorithms, complexity and memory requirements<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Programming and Data Processing<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L1<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compare orders of growth,\njustify search strategies and estimate memory requirements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The cost of a procedure<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001607_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Linear, quadratic and logarithmic example step counts for input sizes 2 to 16, on a logarithmic vertical scale.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Linear, quadratic and logarithmic example\nstep counts for input sizes 2 to 16, on a logarithmic vertical\nscale.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Growth of typical step counts.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>An algorithm is a specified procedure that solves a task in a finite\nnumber of steps, provided its preconditions hold. When searching for a\nvalue in an unsorted list, all\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nentries must be read in the worst case. The cost grows linearly:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn already sorted list permits binary search: a comparison with the\nmiddle element approximately halves the remaining range. In the worst\ncase, at most\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⌊<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⌋<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lfloor\\log_2 n\\rfloor+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncomparisons with list elements are needed (for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin conventional exact binary search). The order is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(\\log n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Search strategy and overall cost<\/h2>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> bisect <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> bisect_left<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>werte <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">19<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">24<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">31<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">42<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">55<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>pos <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> bisect_left(werte, <span class=\"dv\">24<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>gefunden <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> pos <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(werte) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> werte[pos] <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">24<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(pos, gefunden)  <span class=\"co\"># 4 True<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p><code>bisect_left<\/code> determines an insertion position, not\ndirectly whether the value exists; the extra check verifies a match. The\nprocedure requires sorted inputs. If sorting is performed once and\nsearches are then very frequent, the preparation may pay off. For a few\nqueries, linear search may be cheaper. For a list with ongoing additions\nthat was initially unsorted, maintaining its sorted order must be taken\ninto account; insertion into a contiguous array may require shifting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nentries. A hash index is another option, typically with constant\nexpected search cost, but with additional memory and collision\ncosts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Estimating memory requirements<\/h2>\n<p>A dense matrix with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=100\\,000<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrows and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncolumns contains five million values. With 64-bit floating-point\nnumbers, eight bytes per value are needed:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Byte<\/mtext><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=8nd=40\\,000\\,000\\ \\text{Byte}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>That is 40 MB in decimal units, approximately 38.15 MiB in binary\nunits, plus administrative data. A dense pairwise distance matrix of the\nrows, in contrast, would have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvalues, or 80 GB in decimal units. Blockwise processing can avoid\nkeeping all distances in memory simultaneously. It does not necessarily\nsave computational work, however. Actual runtime also depends on\nhardware, data layout and implementation; Big-O describes asymptotic\ngrowth, not seconds.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>How do linear and quadratic steps grow if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis doubled? How many data bytes does the original matrix require with\n<code>float32<\/code>?<\/p>\n<p>Solution: The leading linear count doubles, while the quadratic count\nquadruples. Four bytes per value give 20 million bytes, or 20 MB. The\nlower precision must be appropriate for the task.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/bisect.html\">Python\n3.14, bisect — Array bisection algorithm<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending; access date recorded only in\nsource register (2026-11-20); substantive check not evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M3-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M3-L6 – Data formats, file interfaces and validation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Programming and Data Processing<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L1<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Choose data formats according to\nstructure and check inputs in several stages before analysis.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Representation and contract<\/h2>\n<p>CSV is a row-oriented tabular representation; delimiter, character\nencoding, decimal notation and quoting are part of the interface. JSON\ncan carry nested objects and lists, but has no date type of its own.\nBinary column-oriented formats such as Parquet are suitable for many\nanalytical workloads because they support type information and\ncolumn-wise access. This does not imply universal superiority: small\nexchange files often need an easily inspected text representation. A\nfile format alone does not ensure quality in the application domain.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Parsing and semantic validation<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001546_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Three CSV inputs are subjected to structure, type and range checks; only station 007 is valid.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three CSV inputs are subjected to\nstructure, type and range checks; only station 007 is\nvalid.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Validating a file interface.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A fictional interface supplies a station number and relative humidity\nas a percentage. First the CSV is read syntactically; the type and range\nare then checked. Station identifiers remain strings so that leading\nzeroes are preserved.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> csv<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> io <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> StringIO<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>text <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;station;feuchte<\/span><span class=\"ch\">\\n<\/span><span class=\"st\">007;45<\/span><span class=\"ch\">\\n<\/span><span class=\"st\">008;110<\/span><span class=\"ch\">\\n<\/span><span class=\"st\">009;NA<\/span><span class=\"ch\">\\n<\/span><span class=\"st\">&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>gut, fehler <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [], []<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>reader <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> csv.DictReader(StringIO(text), delimiter<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&quot;;&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> reader.fieldnames <span class=\"op\">!=<\/span> [<span class=\"st\">&quot;station&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;feuchte&quot;<\/span>]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;Unerwartete Spalten&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> zeile <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> reader:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">try<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">set<\/span>(zeile) <span class=\"op\">!=<\/span> {<span class=\"st\">&quot;station&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;feuchte&quot;<\/span>} <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> zeile[<span class=\"st\">&quot;station&quot;<\/span>]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;Struktur oder Kennung&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        f <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">float<\/span>(zeile[<span class=\"st\">&quot;feuchte&quot;<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> f <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;Wertebereich&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        gut.append((zeile[<span class=\"st\">&quot;station&quot;<\/span>], f))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">except<\/span> (<span class=\"pp\">TypeError<\/span>, <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        fehler.append(zeile)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>One row is valid (<code>007<\/code>, 45.0); two are flagged. Under the\nagreed strict interface, <code>NA<\/code> is an error; under another\ncontract it might be an explicitly permitted missing value. Rows\ncontaining errors are kept separately, rather than silently being set to\na limit. Checking syntax, checking data types and assessing plausibility\nin the application domain are different steps. This demonstration is not\na complete parser for arbitrary external data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Data exchange in operation<\/h2>\n<p>A file interface also requires a documented schema version, expected\ncolumns, units and rules for missing data. An API can transfer similar\ndata as JSON; timestamps, status codes, retries and pagination are\ndefined by the contract. For external data, size, nested structures and\nresource consumption are limited. JSON may be read as a data format, not\ninterpreted as executable program text. Hashes can establish that a\nspecific input file is unchanged, not that its content is true.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Add <code>010;0<\/code>, <code>011;100<\/code> and\n<code>012;nan<\/code>. Which new rows pass the range check?<\/p>\n<p>Solution: 0 and 100 are inclusive limits, so both rows are valid.\nAlthough <code>nan<\/code> can be interpreted as a floating-point number,\nit does not satisfy the comparison. After the additions, three rows are\nvalid and three are flagged. A separate error column could distinguish\nbetween type, structure and range errors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/csv.html\">Python 3.14,\ncsv — CSV File Reading and Writing<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending; access date recorded only in source register (2026-11-20);\nsubstantive check not evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/json.html\">Python\n3.14, json — JSON encoder and decoder<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending; access date recorded only in\nsource register (2026-11-20); substantive check not evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://parquet.apache.org/docs/overview/\">Apache Parquet,\nOverview<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending; access date\nrecorded only in source register (2026-11-20); substantive check not\nevidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M3-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M3-L7 – Tests, version control and reproducible environments<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Programming and Data Processing<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/Fundamentals<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L1<\/a>;\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L6<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Define test cases and record the\nprogram, data, execution environment and parameters in a traceable\nway.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Specifying expected behaviour<\/h2>\n<p>A result is not reliable merely because a script finishes without an\nerror message. A function that summarises delivery quantities must\nreturn expected outputs for known inputs. A typical test checks normal\ncases, boundary cases and invalid inputs. For floating-point numbers,\nthe tolerance should be justified in the application domain; exact\nequality is not always meaningful. Tests check the specified properties,\nbut do not automatically prove the absence of all errors.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A minimal automated test<\/h2>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> unittest<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> gesamt(mengen):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">any<\/span>(x <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> mengen):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;Negative Menge&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(mengen)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">class<\/span> TestGesamt(unittest.TestCase):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> test_normal(<span class=\"va\">self<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.assertEqual(gesamt([<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>]), <span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> test_leer(<span class=\"va\">self<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.assertEqual(gesamt([]), <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> test_negativ(<span class=\"va\">self<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">with<\/span> <span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.assertRaises(<span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            gesamt([<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>, <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"va\">__name__<\/span> <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;__main__&quot;<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    unittest.main()<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Saved as <code>test_gesamt.py<\/code>, the file can be run with\n<code>python -m unittest test_gesamt<\/code>. Here, a list of valid\nnumbers is assumed; iterators or non-finite values are not part of this\nsmall contract. Unlike the mean, the sum of an empty list is defined as\nzero, the identity element. Integration tests additionally check\ninteractions, such as the process from reading a file through to\noutputting an indicator.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reproducible execution<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001683_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Code, data, environment and parameters together form the inputs of a checked analysis run.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Code, data, environment and parameters\ntogether form the inputs of a checked analysis run.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Components of reproducible analyses.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Version control documents changes to source code. A Git commit\nidentifies a specific project state; a tag can mark an approved version.\nData that are not stored in Git need their own version or content hash.\nAn isolated environment (<code>python -m venv .venv<\/code>) separates\nproject dependencies. For a repeat run, document the Python version,\npackage versions including transitive dependencies where possible,\noperating system, parameters, data version and launch command.\n<code>pip freeze<\/code> can record installed versions, but does not\nreplace a complete cross-platform lock with artefact hashes. A fixed\nrandom seed can control random sequences; parallel execution, hardware\nor changed libraries may nevertheless produce different results.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Write an additional test for <code>[0, 0]<\/code>. Why is it not\nsufficient to archive only the commit?<\/p>\n<p>Solution: <code>self.assertEqual(gesamt([0, 0]), 0)<\/code> checks two\nexplicit zero quantities. The commit may contain neither the input file\nused nor the library versions, parameters or system environment. These\ndata must be documented separately and associated unambiguously with the\nanalysis.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/unittest.html\">Python\n3.14, unittest — Unit testing framework<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending; access date recorded only in\nsource register (2026-11-20); substantive check not evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3.14/tutorial/venv.html\">Python\n3.14, Virtual Environments and Packages, Ch. 12<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending; access date recorded only in\nsource register (2026-11-20); substantive check not evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Basics-Recording-Changes-to-the-Repository\">Pro\nGit, 2.2 Recording Changes to the Repository<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending; access date recorded only in\nsource register (2026-11-20); substantive check not evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M3-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M3-L8 – Functional programming and concurrent data processing<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M3 – Programming and Data Processing<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Elective specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L1<\/a>\nto <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L7<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Formulate processing of data\npartitions with few side effects and distinguish concurrency from\nparallelism.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Functions as building blocks<\/h2>\n<p>A pure function returns the same value for the same input and does\nnot change any externally observable state. A data process can often be\ndivided into small functions: validate, convert, aggregate. Side effects\nsuch as file access or logging are kept at clearly named interfaces.\nThis makes testing easier; complete purity is not always practical in\nreal programs. Generators can supply data incrementally, so not every\nintermediate value needs to be in memory at the same time.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Processing data partitions independently<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001654_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Two blocks supply sums and counts, which are combined to obtain 36/5 = 7.2.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two blocks supply sums and counts, which\nare combined to obtain 36/5 = 7.2.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Partial statistics and combined reduction.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A series of measurements is divided into two blocks. For each block,\nwe calculate its sum and count. A thread pool can manage independent\ntasks concurrently; here, <code>map<\/code> returns the results in input\norder.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> concurrent.futures <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> ThreadPoolExecutor<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> teile_statistik(block):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(block), <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(block)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>bloecke <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>], [<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">14<\/span>]]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">with<\/span> ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>) <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> pool:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    teile <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">list<\/span>(pool.<span class=\"bu\">map<\/span>(teile_statistik, bloecke))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>summe <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(s <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> s, n <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> teile)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>anzahl <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(n <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> s, n <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> teile)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(summe <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> anzahl)  <span class=\"co\"># 7.2<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The partial results are (12, 3) and (24, 2). They yield the correct\nmean of 36/5. The unweighted mean of the block means would be 8 and\ntherefore incorrect. An empty overall input would need to be handled\nbefore division. Blockwise summation of floating-point values may change\nthe rounding. The reduction order can therefore be part of\nreproducibility.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concurrency, parallelism and costs<\/h2>\n<p>Concurrency allows several tasks to be organised with overlapping\nactivity; parallelism means actual simultaneous execution. Threads are\noften suitable for waiting during input/output operations. For\nCPU-intensive Python code, separate processes are often more suitable in\na conventional CPython build with the GIL; library operations may\nrelease the GIL, and free-threaded builds have different properties.\nProcesses incur costs for startup, communication and data transfer.\nShared mutable state can cause race conditions if the result depends on\nthe execution order. Independent blocks and local results avoid many\nsuch problems. The example pool demonstrates the interface, not a speed\nimprovement.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>A third block contains <code>[20]<\/code>. Determine the overall mean\nwithout combining the raw data. Why is a thread pool not automatically\nfaster?<\/p>\n<p>Solution: The new partial pair is (20, 1). Sum 56, count 6, mean\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>56<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>9.33<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">56/6\\approx9.33<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor small tasks, management overhead may dominate; for CPU-intensive\nPython code, additional threads may also be constrained by the GIL.\nComparative measurements using representative data determine the\nanswer.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3.14/howto/functional.html\">Python\n3.14, Functional Programming HOWTO<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending; access date recorded only in source register (2026-11-20);\nsubstantive check not evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/concurrent.futures.html\">Python\n3.14, concurrent.futures — Launching parallel tasks<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> –\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending; access date recorded only in\nsource register (2026-11-20); substantive check not evidenced.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
